      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0,\\,\\text{E}[Y]=0.4,\\,\\text{SD}[X]=2,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]=0.8,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0.1+(-2)\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.2+(-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & +2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.4-(0)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4}{2\\cdot 0.8} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
