    "q-148425": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148425",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}(x{y}^{3}+2), & \\,0\\le 2x\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{52}{35},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2575,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.4389,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{2x}^{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{3}{10}(x{y}^{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{2x}^{2}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+2xy)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}{y}^{5}+x{y}^{2}]}_{y=2x}^{y=2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{32}{5}{x}^{2}+4x-\\frac{32}{5}{x}^{7}-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}{[\\frac{32}{15}{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{8}-{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{35}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}-(\\frac{3}{7})(\\frac{52}{35}) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{490}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{31}{490})}{(0.2575)\\cdot (0.4389)} \\\\  & ≈0.56,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
